[38;2;124;124;124m**Theorem.** Let $G$ be a compact metrizable abelian group. Then the characters [0m[38;2;124;124;124mof $G$ separate points: for every $g \in G$ with $g \neq 0$, there exists a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuous homomorphism $\chi: G \to \mathbb{T}$ (where $\mathbb{T} = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$) such that $\chi(g) \neq 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Proof.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe use additive notation throughout. Let $\mu$ denote the normalized Haar [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeasure on $G$ ($\mu(G) = 1$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 1 (Faithfulness of the regular representation).** For $g \in G$, let [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$L_g: L^2(G) \to L^2(G)$ be the left translation operator $(L_g f)(x) = f(x - [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mg)$. This is a unitary operator. We claim $L_g = I$ implies $g = 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $g \neq 0$, since $G$ is Hausdorff, there exists an open neighborhood $U$ of [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$0$ with $g \notin U$. Set $f = \mathbf{1}_U \in L^2(G)$. Then $L_g f = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbf{1}_{U + g}$. Since $0 \in U$ and $g \in U + g$ (because $0 \in U$), but [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$g \notin U$, the symmetric difference $U \triangle (U+g)$ contains the nonempty[0m[38;2;124;124;124mopen set $U \setminus (U+g)$ or $(U+g) \setminus U$; in either case it has [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpositive $\mu$-measure (being a nonempty open set in a compact group with Haar [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeasure positive on open sets). Hence $\|L_g f - f\|_2 > 0$, so $L_g \neq I$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 2 (Convolution operators are compact).** For $\phi \in L^2(G)$, define [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$T_\phi: L^2(G) \to L^2(G)$ by $(T_\phi f)(x) = \int_G \phi(y) f(x-y)\,d\mu(y) =[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(\phi * f)(x)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe operator $T_\phi$ is Hilbert-Schmidt: its integral kernel is $K(x,y) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\phi(x-y)$, and[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\|T_\phi\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2 = \iint_{G \times G} |\phi(x-y)|[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m^2\,d\mu(x)\,d\mu(y) = \|\phi\|_2^2 \cdot \mu(G) = \|\phi\|_2^2 < \infty.$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mHence $T_\phi$ is a compact operator. Moreover, $T_\phi$ commutes with all [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mtranslations: $L_g T_\phi = T_\phi L_g$ for all $g \in G$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 3 (Self-adjoint convolution operators).** Choose a nonzero $\phi \in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mL^2(G)$ that is real-valued and even ($\phi(-x) = \phi(x)$ a.e.). For instance, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mtake $\phi = \mathbf{1}_U * \mathbf{1}_{-U}$ for a nonempty open set $U$; this [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mis real, even, non-negative, and nonzero. Then $T_\phi$ is self-adjoint (since $[0m[38;2;124;124;124m\overline{\phi(-x)} = \phi(x)$), compact, and positive semidefinite.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $T_\phi \neq 0$ and $T_\phi \geq 0$, the operator $T_\phi$ has at least [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mone positive eigenvalue $\lambda > 0$ with a finite-dimensional eigenspace [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$E_\lambda \neq \{0\}$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 4 (Eigenspaces are spanned by characters).** Since $T_\phi$ commutes with[0m[38;2;124;124;124mevery $L_g$, each eigenspace $E_\lambda$ ($\lambda \neq 0$) is invariant under [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mall $L_g$. The restriction $\{L_g|_{E_\lambda}\}_{g \in G}$ is a finite-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdimensional unitary representation of the abelian group $G$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince $G$ is abelian, the operators $\{L_g|_{E_\lambda}\}$ form a commuting [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfamily of unitary (hence normal) operators on the finite-dimensional space [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$E_\lambda$. By the spectral theorem for commuting normal operators, $E_\lambda$[0m[38;2;124;124;124mdecomposes into simultaneous eigenspaces:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$E_\lambda = \bigoplus_{\alpha} V_\alpha, \quad V_\alpha = \{v \in E_\lambda : [0m[38;2;124;124;124mL_g v = \chi_\alpha(g)\, v \;\forall\, g \in G\},$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mwhere $\chi_\alpha(g) \in S^1$ (unit circle, since $L_g$ is unitary).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mEach $\chi_\alpha: G \to S^1$ is a group homomorphism (since $L_{g+h} = L_g [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mL_h$). It is continuous: for $v \in V_\alpha \setminus \{0\}$, we have $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\chi_\alpha(g) = \langle L_g v, v\rangle / \|v\|^2$, and $g \mapsto L_g v$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontinuous in $L^2$ (a standard property of the regular representation), so $g [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mapsto \chi_\alpha(g)$ is continuous. Thus $\chi_\alpha$ is a character of $G$.[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mEach $V_\alpha$ is 1-dimensional (spanned by any nonzero $v \in V_\alpha$, which[0m[38;2;124;124;124mas an $L^2$ function is a scalar multiple of the character $\chi_\alpha$). So [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$E_\lambda$ is spanned by characters.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 5 (Characters span a dense subspace).** Let $V = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\overline{\operatorname{span}}\{\chi : \chi \text{ is a character of } G\} [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\subseteq L^2(G)$. We show $V = L^2(G)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFrom Step 4, for any real even $\phi \in L^2(G)$, the image $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\overline{\operatorname{im}(T_\phi)} = \ker(T_\phi)^\perp$ is contained in $V$ [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(since $\operatorname{im}(T_\phi)$ is the closure of the span of eigenspaces for[0m[38;2;124;124;124mnonzero eigenvalues, each spanned by characters).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSuppose $f \in V^\perp$. Then $f \perp \overline{\operatorname{im}(T_\phi)}$ for[0m[38;2;124;124;124mevery real even $\phi$, so $f \in \ker(T_\phi)$ (since $T_\phi$ is self-adjoint)[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfor every real even $\phi$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow let $\{\phi_n\}$ be an approximate identity consisting of real, even, non-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnegative $L^2$ functions (e.g., $\phi_n = c_n \mathbf{1}_{B_n} * \mathbf{1}_{-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mB_n}$ where $B_n$ are balls of radius $1/n$ around $0$ and $c_n$ are normalizing[0m[38;2;124;124;124mconstants). Then $T_{\phi_n} f = \phi_n * f \to f$ in $L^2$ (standard property [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mof approximate identities on compact groups). But $T_{\phi_n} f = 0$ for all [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$n$, so $f = 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mHence $V^\perp = \{0\}$, and $V = L^2(G)$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 6 (Characters separate points).** Let $g \in G$ with $g \neq 0$. By Step [0m[38;2;124;124;124m1, $L_g \neq I$ on $L^2(G)$. Suppose for contradiction that $\chi(g) = 1$ for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mevery character $\chi$. Then $L_g \chi = \chi(g) \cdot \chi = \chi$ for every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcharacter $\chi$, so $L_g = I$ on $\operatorname{span}\{\chi\}$, which is dense [0m[38;2;124;124;124min $L^2(G)$ by Step 5. Since $L_g$ is continuous (unitary), $L_g = I$ on all of [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$L^2(G)$, contradicting Step 1.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, there exists a character $\chi$ with $\chi(g) \neq 1$ (equivalently, [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$\chi(g) \neq 0$ in additive notation for $\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Remark.** The metrizability hypothesis is not needed; the proof works for any [0m[38;2;124;124;124mcompact abelian (Hausdorff) group. Metrizability ensures $\widehat{G}$ is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcountable, but the separation property holds in general.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Conclusion.** A compact metrizable abelian group has enough characters to [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mseparate points. $\blacksquare$[0m

